from pathlib import Path
import re

path = Path('docs/research_paper_ieee_updated.tex')
s = path.read_text(encoding='utf-8')

def once(old, new):
    global s
    n = s.count(old)
    if n != 1:
        raise ValueError(f'Expected one match, found {n}: {old[:90]}')
    s = s.replace(old, new, 1)

once(r'\title{Mitigating Catastrophic Forgetting and Exploitability in Competitive Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning via Dual-Archive Co-Evolution}',
     r'\title{Route Diversity and Exploitability in Competitive Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning}')
once('Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning, Self-Play, Catastrophic Forgetting, Nash Equilibrium, Empirical Game-Theoretic Analysis, Potential-Based Reward Shaping, Zero-Sum Games.',
     'Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning, Self-Play, Route Diversity, Exploitability, Potential-Based Reward Shaping, Zero-Sum Games.')

abstract = r'''\begin{abstract}
In multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), agents learn while affecting one another. In self-play, each agent faces an opponent that is also changing, so a route that worked before may stop working. This paper studies that problem in the Three Bridges Arena, a grid game where an Attacker crosses one of three bridges to reach a target while a Defender tries to catch it. We compare direct self-play with Dual-Archive Co-Evolution, which also trains agents against saved versions of earlier opponents. The agents use symmetric potential-based reward shaping with a discount factor of 0.99; this keeps the shaping rewards for the two players equal and opposite. In 150 paired evaluations, direct self-play used the North bridge in 46.0 percent of crossings and the South bridge in 16.1 percent, and 17.3 percent of episodes timed out. The archive-trained policy had route entropy of 1.567 bits, or 98.9 percent of the maximum for three routes, and no timeouts in this evaluation. Its measured exploitability was 0.140, compared with 0.160 for direct self-play. The worst-case security score changed from -0.207 to -0.187. The pooled win-rate difference was -0.6 percent and was not statistically significant. These results suggest that route use and exploitability can help compare strategies. They do not directly test whether an agent has forgotten a previously learned route.
\end{abstract}'''
s, n = re.subn(r'\\begin\{abstract\}.*?\\end\{abstract\}', lambda _m: abstract, s, count=1, flags=re.S)
if n != 1:
    raise ValueError('Could not replace the abstract exactly once')

once('Competitive Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) has driven major breakthroughs across complex game domains, including chess, Go, poker, and real-time strategy environments \\cite{silver2016mastering, vinyals2019alphastar, berner2019dota}. In these settings, self-play serves as an automated curriculum: agents discover counter-strategies by competing against concurrently learning copies of themselves.',
     'In multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), several decision-making agents act in the same environment. In self-play, agents learn by playing against one another. Each agent changes its behavior as it learns, so the opponent is also changing during training. Self-play has produced strong results in games such as Go, StarCraft, and Dota 2 \\cite{silver2016mastering, vinyals2019alphastar, berner2019dota}.')
once('Despite empirical successes, unanchored self-play exhibits well-known game-theoretic vulnerabilities \\cite{balduzzi2019open}. Because both agents update their parameters simultaneously, the optimization landscape is non-stationary from the perspective of either learner. This non-stationarity frequently triggers the evolutionary dynamic known as the \\textit{Red Queen Effect} \\cite{cliff1995tracking}: agents must continuously adapt simply to maintain relative performance parity. In games characterized by intransitive strategy structures (e.g., spatial variants of Rock-Paper-Scissors), independent gradient descent typically induces closed behavioral cycling rather than monotonic convergence toward a minimax optimal Nash equilibrium.',
     'When both agents learn at the same time, each agent faces a changing opponent. Researchers call this a non-stationary learning problem. In some games, strategies can cycle: one behavior is answered by another, which is later answered in turn. This pattern is sometimes compared with the Red Queen effect \\cite{balduzzi2019open, cliff1995tracking}. The comparison is an analogy; this grid game is not a biological model.')
once('Closely coupled with policy cycling is the pathology of \\textit{catastrophic forgetting} \\cite{mccloskey1989catastrophic}. When an opponent shifts defensive focus from Route A to Route B, policy gradients penalize Route A and incentivize Route B. Consequently, gradient updates alter neural network representations to specialize against the immediate adversary, overwriting policy weights that successfully traversed Route A. When the opponent subsequently shifts back to Route B, the agent is re-exploited, causing persistent cyclic forgetting.',
     'A related concern is catastrophic forgetting: a neural network may lose an earlier skill while learning a new one \\cite{mccloskey1989catastrophic}. In this project, we use bridge choices as an indirect measure of route diversity. A change in route frequency alone does not prove that an agent has forgotten how to use a route.')
once('To overcome policy cycling and catastrophic forgetting in this setting, we make the following contributions:',
     'This paper describes the arena, compares self-play with archive training, and reports the following measurements:')
once('\\item \\textbf{Mathematical Formulation and Invariant Guarantee:} We formalize the spatial bottleneck interaction as a finite zero-sum Markov game. We integrate discounted potential-based reward shaping with both target-advance and pursuit-evasion components, preserving the strict zero-sum invariant ($R_A + R_D \\equiv 0.000000$) while guaranteeing invariance of the Nash equilibrium under discount factor $\\gamma = 0.99$ (Ng et al. \\cite{ng1999policy}; Devlin and Kudenko \\cite{devlin2011theoretical}).',
     '\\item \\textbf{Game and reward design:} We describe the grid game and use symmetric reward shaping. The two shaping rewards are equal in size and opposite in sign, so their sum is zero. Earlier work gives conditions under which potential-based shaping preserves policies or equilibria (Ng et al. \\cite{ng1999policy}; Devlin and Kudenko \\cite{devlin2011theoretical}).')
once('\\item \\textbf{Shannon Route Entropy Metric for Spatial Forgetting:} We establish an information-theoretic metric measuring the deviation of empirical river crossings from the theoretical maximum mixed strategy entropy ($H_{\\max} = \\log_2(3) \\approx 1.585$ bits).',
     '\\item \\textbf{Route diversity measurement:} We use Shannon entropy to summarize how evenly successful crossings are spread across the three bridges. This describes route use; it is not a direct test of forgetting.')
once('\\item \\textbf{Empirical Validation:} Across 150 standardized paired evaluation seeds, we show that Dual-Archive co-evolution prevents catastrophic route collapse (achieving $1.567$ bits, $98.9\\%$ of theoretical maximum entropy) with 0.0\\% timeouts, while reducing empirical exploitability from $\\delta = 0.160$ to $0.140$ and raising the worst-case security floor from $-0.207$ to $-0.187$.',
     '\\item \\textbf{Evaluation:} In 150 paired evaluations, the archive-trained policy used the three routes more evenly and had fewer timeouts than the direct self-play policy. Its measured exploitability was 0.140 versus 0.160, and its worst-case security score was -0.187 versus -0.207. These results are limited to the policies and opponents tested here.')

